Title: The Impact of Population Ageing on Public Finances in the EU-27

Abstract: This paper analyses the impact of demographic ageing on age-related public expenditure in the EU-27 from 1992 to 2024. The analysis covers healthcare, long-term care (LTC), and pensions. The results reveal three patterns. First, demographic effects are heterogeneous across countries, driven by short-run responses that vary by spending category: limited in healthcare, absent in LTC. Second, advanced ageing (80+) matters more for long-run dynamics, especially in healthcare. Third, Central and Eastern European economies show weaker or delayed responses, while Western and Nordic countries adjust more strongly, especially in healthcare and LTC rather than pensions, reflecting the decisive role of institutional design.

Classification-JEL: E60, E62, H20, H51, H55, J11, J14

Keywords: Ageing, public expenditure, demographic change, European Union

